I have ceased to fear the RNG.  It may play some small role, but the
biggest obstacle in NetHack is your mind.

I had an adventurer randomly chosen.  I got a neutral Valkyrie.  On
the first floor, her kitten picked up a +2 orcish helm.  In the next
room over, someone had left splint mail lying around.  Downstairs,
she found leather gloves, also +2.  Result: halfway through level 2,
her AC was down to -6.

Having heard about the RNG and its seeming gifts, I expected my
adventurer to meet a quick end at the pincers of a swarm of soldier
ants, or something similar.  Instead, the good fortune continued.
Nothing particularly dangerous appeared all the way to the bottom of
the Gnomish Mines.  A bones pile on the Oracle level provided a magic 
marker.  A potion of holy water randomly appeared, so I had her
dilute some fruit juice and create six more.  She found a wand of
polymorph, which transformed her faithful housecat into a fearsome
silver dragon.  Back in the main dungeon, there randomly appeared not
one but two leprechaun halls.

I sent the Valkyrie back to Minetown to buy protection.  Her
contribution so pleased the priest of Tyr that he granted her four
points of protection on the first donation.

And the good fortune continued.  At least four wraiths appeared
randomly and left corpses.  Odin granted her Mjollnir on the first
sacrifice after converting an altar.  So she was able to enter the quest
after exploring only down to level 16.  Lord Surtur died with even less
trouble than usual.

I did a bad job of setting up stashes, which meant that she had trouble
avoiding being burdened, but still had to run up and down for things
left in various chests and boxes spread from the Mines to the Rogue
level.  Somehow or other, her stack of holy water disappeared.  My
suspicion is that a peaceful centaur looted a chest which I forgot to
lock.  So I sent her back to Minetown to make more.

She was burdened for most of the trip, as I wanted her to identify, use,
dilute, bless, or write a large number of potions and scrolls.  Food was
short- she had already climbed up and down several times.  Berbak's
delicatessen had long since been bought out and Berbak himself fed to
her pet dragon.  But a squad a Mordor orcs attacked Minetown, so with
some to eat and some to offer, she soon had the altar converted and 10
potions of water blessed.

In retrospect, it was a waste of a prayer, with no food in the pack,
just above hungry, all the nearby monsters killed, and nothing edible
in any nearby stash.

I could have sent her farther down to some mine levels that hadn't been
completely cleared, but I ignored the problem and made for a K-ration
in a chest 10 levels below in the main dungeon.

It was only just out of the Mines that she began to faint from lack of
food.  Can't pray, better kill some monsters.  The pet dragon eagerly
helped kill the monsters, but he was hungry too, and it's hard to beat
a dragon to the corpses of his own victims when you keep fainting.

Finally, when she was fainting just about every move, she got between
the dragon and some monsters in a corridor.  She managed to kill a
jaguar and move over its corpse, but an elf mummy was attacking, so I
had her hit it first so she could eat in peace.  Instead of attacking,
she died of starvation.

A level 15 Valkyrie, with AC about -16 and a good variety of intrinsics,
Mjollnir at +3 with plenty of holy water, blank scrolls, and an unused
magic marker, without having gone anywhere near Medusa's level yet,
killed by stupidity, no credit to the RNG.

It was about an hour later that I realized she had been carrying three
lizard corpses.


